  <section class="section">
    <h2>How Ohio got the power to choose</h2>
    <p class="sub">For decades, one utility owned everything. That changed in 2001 when Ohio opened its electricity market to retail competition.</p>
    <div class="grid2">
      <div class="panel">
        <h3>Ohio's 2001 deregulation</h3>
        <p>In <strong>1999</strong>, Ohio passed <strong>Senate Bill 3</strong>, the law that broke up the old utility monopoly. On <strong>January 1, 2002</strong>, the market opened: for the first time, most Ohio homes and businesses could choose their own retail electricity supplier, while the local utility continued delivering the power.</p>
        <p class="muted">The law split the business into two roles — the utility that owns poles and wires and delivers power, and the retail suppliers (Certified Retail Electric Suppliers, or CRES) that compete to sell you electricity.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel">
        <h3>PUCO and your protections</h3>
        <p>The <strong>Public Utilities Commission of Ohio (PUCO)</strong> regulates utilities and the competitive market, charged with keeping power reliable and ensuring fair competition. Senate Bill 3 locked in consumer protections every supplier must honor:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Safe, reliable, and reasonably priced electricity</li>
          <li>Clear, accurate billing and honest plan disclosures</li>
          <li>Accurate metering and protection of your private information</li>
          <li>The right to switch suppliers without service interruption</li>
          <li>Access to tools like Energy Choice Ohio to compare offers</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>How deregulated Ohio electricity works</h2>
    <p class="sub">Two different companies touch your power. Knowing which does what is the whole game.</p>
    <div class="grid2">
      <div class="panel">
        <h3>Your retail supplier (CRES)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Who they are:</strong> Certified Retail Electric Suppliers like Ambit Energy that sell you an electricity plan.</li>
          <li><strong>What they set:</strong> your rate, your contract term, and your billing.</li>
          <li><strong>Your choice:</strong> you pick the supplier, and you can switch when you want.</li>
          <li><strong>Why ZIP matters:</strong> the plans and prices a supplier can offer change by ZIP and utility area.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="muted">This is the part you control. Picking the right supplier and plan is where the savings come from.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel">
        <h3>Your local utility (delivery company)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Who they are:</strong> AEP Ohio, Duke Energy Ohio, FirstEnergy, or Dayton Power &amp; Light.</li>
          <li><strong>What they do:</strong> own the poles, wires, and meters and restore outages.</li>
          <li><strong>Assigned, not chosen:</strong> your utility is set by your address. Switching suppliers never changes it.</li>
          <li><strong>Same for everyone:</strong> delivery charges are regulated and identical across all suppliers in that area.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="muted">Because the utility is the same no matter who you buy from, your power stays on through a switch and outages are handled the same way.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Understand the details before you switch</h2>
    <p class="sub">The pieces that decide what you actually pay, in plain English.</p>

    <details class="accordion"><summary>Why electricity rates vary by ZIP code</summary>
      <p>Every supplier asks for your ZIP first because the plans and prices they can offer change from place to place:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Utility delivery charges differ between service areas.</li>
        <li>Local competition shifts the range of prices on offer.</li>
        <li>Usage patterns vary between urban and rural areas.</li>
        <li>Wholesale energy costs differ across utility load zones.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Even neighboring cities can land on different rates. That is why a real comparison starts with your exact address and ZIP.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="accordion"><summary>How to read your electricity plan</summary>
      <p>Before you sign up, understand these key details:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Energy charge (rate per kWh):</strong> the main price you pay for electricity.</li>
        <li><strong>Utility delivery charges:</strong> regulated charges set by your local utility for maintaining poles and wires.</li>
        <li><strong>Contract length:</strong> how long you are locked in. Shorter is more flexible, longer can lock in savings.</li>
        <li><strong>Early termination fee:</strong> what it costs if you leave before the term ends.</li>
        <li><strong>Any monthly base fees:</strong> extra charges just for having an account.</li>
        <li><strong>Renewable content:</strong> the percentage of energy from renewable sources, if any.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Always ask the supplier to show you the plan details before you enroll.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="accordion"><summary>The plan types you will see</summary>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Fixed-rate:</strong> the rate stays the same for the whole term. Best for budgeting and stability.</li>
        <li><strong>Variable or indexed:</strong> the rate can change month to month. Bills can spike, so compare carefully.</li>
        <li><strong>Time-of-use:</strong> free nights or free weekends. Best if you can shift usage into the free window.</li>
        <li><strong>Green/Renewable:</strong> matched to wind or solar generation. Often priced the same as standard plans in Ohio.</li>
      </ul>
    </details>

    <details class="accordion"><summary>Moving to Ohio? Setting up electricity</summary>
      <p>Setting up power in a deregulated part of Ohio is quick. Have these ready:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Your new address and ZIP code</li>
        <li>Your planned move-in date</li>
        <li>Your ID or SSN for a soft credit check</li>
        <li>An estimate of your usage: apartment, townhome, or full house</li>
      </ul>
      <p>You can start or switch service online in a few minutes.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="accordion"><summary>Electricity for apartments</summary>
      <p>Smaller homes do best with plans built for lower usage:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Fixed-rate plans that do not penalize low usage under 600-800 kWh.</li>
        <li>Shorter terms that match your lease length.</li>
        <li>Plans without high monthly base fees.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Compare your exact usage level when shopping.</p>
    </details>
  </section>

