    <h2>Ohio electricity questions</h2>
    <details class="accordion"><summary>How do I know if I can choose a supplier in Ohio?</summary>
      <p>Most of Ohio has a deregulated market where you can choose your supplier. If you live in areas served by AEP Ohio, Duke Energy Ohio, FirstEnergy, or Dayton Power &amp; Light in their competitive zones, you likely have a choice. Enter your ZIP above to confirm.</p></details>
    <details class="accordion"><summary>Will my electricity get disconnected when I switch suppliers?</summary>
      <p>No. Switching suppliers in Ohio is designed to be seamless. As long as your account is in good standing, your power stays on while the switch is processed.</p></details>
    <details class="accordion"><summary>Do I choose my utility?</summary>
      <p>No. Your utility is assigned based on your physical location. You choose the retail supplier (CRES), but the utility that maintains the grid stays the same.</p></details>
    <details class="accordion"><summary>Are delivery charges the same for every supplier?</summary>
      <p>Yes. Utility delivery charges are regulated by PUCO and are identical across all suppliers in that service area.</p></details>
    <details class="accordion"><summary>What contract length is best?</summary>
      <p>Many Ohio customers choose a 12-month fixed plan for balance and predictability. When rates are favorable, longer terms like 24 or 36 months can lock in protection for a longer period.</p></details>
    <details class="accordion"><summary>Can I switch back if I do not like my new supplier?</summary>
      <p>Yes. You can switch suppliers at any time if you are not under contract, or you can wait for your contract to end. Switching is free and happens automatically without service interruption.</p></details>
